{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1517151-question-on-celerity-and-multiple-attacks-in-vda20","id":1517151,"title":"Question on Celerity and multiple attacks in VDA20","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-07-04T03:40:11-07:00","id":1517151,"title":"Question on Celerity and multiple attacks in VDA20","content":"Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> In VDA 20, p.202, Celerity's power for multiple actions is explained this way:<br /> <br /> &quot;Additionally, they may reflexively spend one point of blood to ignore difficulty penalties for multiple actions up to their Celerity dots for the turn. For example, a character with Celerity 3 makes three actions before suffering +1 difficulty for multiple actions. This does not mitigate the dice pool reduction, however. A number of these additional actions equal to half their Celerity (round up) may be attack actions. See p. 322 for rules on multiple actions.&quot;<br /> <br /> How does this interact with the additional attacks offered by some discipline powers (ex.: Serpentis 2 for the tongue and 4 for the tail, pp.252-3), or those available to animals with different attacks (who may be ghouled and learn Celerity) (p.396: &quot;Animals have listed attacks. This does not necessarily mean they inherently receive additional actions, but they may split their action to make multiple attacks if they have different modes of attack available. This occurs like a normal multiple action (see p. 322).&quot;)?<br /> <br /> Specifically, I wonder whether the additional attacks due to natural weapons and Serpentis can be done with the non-attack actions of Celerity. Ex: Celerity 2 gives you 2 actions without difficulty penalty, one of which can be an(y) attack. Given the exception of having additional attacks like the tongue, can one do a normal attack and then a tongue attack, both without difficulty penalty? If the answer is no, how does one handle the order of the actions? Would one do, for example, 1 attack with Celerity without penalty, 1 attack with the tongue with penalty, and then a dodge or parry without penalty again?<br /> <br /> Thanks for the help, especially if it comes from those who were involved in the project.<br /> <br /> EDIT: the VDA20 Companion, p.120, also says:<br /> &quot;If a character using two weapons earns one advantage: In multiple actions, she may attack twice in a row (but no more), instead of waiting for the turn to cycle around as usual. Two weapons are ideal for the Riposte maneuver (see V20 Dark Ages, p. 347) so you might give double weapon users a -1 difficulty advantage, cancelling out the usual +1 difficulty penalty for the maneuver (but not the difficulty penalty for multiple actions) as long as the defensive maneuver used to set it up is a parry&quot;<br /> <br /> How does that work with Celerity?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 07-04-2023, 07:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T07:03:11-07:00","id":1517156,"title":"","content":"It's been a minute since I played DAV20, but here's my understanding. Like everything with actions and action economy, you declare actions up front, <i>then</i> resolve. Specific to Celerity, this is so the ST and players can determine difficulties and dice pools up front. Celerity in DAV20 <i>does not</i> grant free actions; it modifies declared multiple actions.<br /> <br /> Discipline powers that modify attacks don't impact this, unless there's a contingent aspect to that attack. Or to put it another way, it requires an action to use a tongue lash, bite, or tail strike as per Serpentis. The &quot;additional attack&quot; language is there to clarify they can be used alongside other forms of attack, like for example if a PC wants to stab one NPC and tongue lash another. That would still count as two actions, and the relevant declarations would need to be made and modifiers applied.<br /> <br /> It does not require an action to drink as part of a tongue lash, or initiate grapple as part of a bite, as those are automatic and contingent upon successful attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 07-04-2023, 10:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T08:14:59-07:00","id":1517161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1517151-question-on-celerity-and-multiple-attacks-in-vda20?p=1517156#post1517156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's been a minute since I played DAV20, but here's my understanding. Like everything with actions and action economy, you declare actions up front, <i>then</i> resolve. Specific to Celerity, this is so the ST and players can determine difficulties and dice pools up front. Celerity in DAV20 <i>does not</i> grant free actions; it modifies declared multiple actions.</div> </div> </div> </div>To my understanding, in this edition one only declares that they are going to perform multiple actions rather than a single standard action. One doesn't need to declare which actions, first because it's not written that they have to, and second because the actions will change depending on the need to dodge / block / parry and what may follow that, like a riposte maneuver, and no penalty is mentioned for changing one's declared action.<br /> \u0130n other editions, yes, one would declare actions and face a penalty if they changed them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1517151-question-on-celerity-and-multiple-attacks-in-vda20?p=1517156#post1517156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Discipline powers that modify attacks don't impact this, unless there's a contingent aspect to that attack. Or to put it another way, it requires an action to use a tongue lash, bite, or tail strike as per Serpentis. The &quot;additional attack&quot; language is there to clarify they can be used alongside other forms of attack, like for example if a PC wants to stab one NPC and tongue lash another. That would still count as two actions, and the relevant declarations would need to be made and modifiers applied.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u0130 think we're saying the same thing, minus the declaration part, but just to be explicit in case we are NOT saying the same thing: in this edition the default is that, even when using multiple actions, one can only do one attack action. The other actions are non-attack actions, like defensive or social or mental actions. Two exceptions are 1) the availability of additional attacks of a special kind, which are mentioned specifically in the three cases \u0130 mentioned (Serpentis, animals with multiple natural weapons and wielding two weapons) plus one of Abombwe's powers; 2) Celerity, which \u0130 quoted above and differs from the first exception in that you can do ANY attack, while with the additional weapons you have to use that particular weapon.<br /> Ex: with a sword and Serpentis 2 you can do a bite and tongue lash, or a sword strike and a tongue lash, but not a sword strike and a bite, because only the tongue lash is an &quot;additional attack&quot; for Serpentis 2. But with a sword and Celerity 3 you can do two sword strikes. With Celerity 1 or 2 you can only do your normal sword strike, but with reduced penalties if you declare multiple actions to be able to dodge or parry as well.<br /> <br /> Having said all that, \u0130 haven't understood your conclusion in terms of how Celerity (exception 2 above) and exception 1 above would interact. Sorry, could you clarify?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1517151-question-on-celerity-and-multiple-attacks-in-vda20?p=1517156#post1517156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u0130t does not require an action to drink as part of a tongue lash, or initiate grapple as part of a bite, as those are automatic and contingent upon successful attack.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T18:08:59-07:00","id":1517184,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1517151-question-on-celerity-and-multiple-attacks-in-vda20?p=1517161#post1517161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To my understanding, in this edition one only declares that they are going to perform multiple actions rather than a single standard action. One doesn't need to declare which actions...</div> </div> </div> </div>You still have to declare to determine target number and dice pools for each action.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> ...Two exceptions are 1) the availability of additional attacks of a special kind, which are mentioned specifically in the three cases \u0130 mentioned (Serpentis, animals with multiple natural weapons and wielding two weapons)...Having said all that, \u0130 haven't understood your conclusion in terms of how Celerity (exception 2 above) and exception 1 above would interact. Sorry, could you clarify? </div> </div> </div>They're not special in terms of action economy, and they're not free actions. They're just an additional combat <i>option</i> upon which one may spend an action. You may make a weapon attack or claws, if applicable, and a tongue lash or tail swipe without Celerity, <i>so long as you are declaring two actions to do both</i>.<br /> <br /> As far as Celerity, what I mean by &quot;they do not interact&quot; is Celerity itself supersedes the default rules on multiple attacks. It's higher on the &quot;order of operations&quot;, so to speak. With Celerity 1 or 2, you may make two attacks per round; with Celerity 3 or 4, you may make three; with Celerity 5, you may make four. The one attack you get by default, plus half your Celerity rating rounded up.<br /> <br /> When using Celerity, multiple weapons or additional natural weapons does not increase this cap. So if a character is, say, dual wielding, Celerity 1 or 2 does nothing in terms of total number of potential attacks; it isn't until Celerity 3 you can make a third attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T18:45:12-07:00","id":1517187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1517151-question-on-celerity-and-multiple-attacks-in-vda20?p=1517184#post1517184\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You still have to declare to determine target number and dice pools for each action.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> They're not special in terms of action economy, and they're not free actions. They're just an additional combat <i>option</i> upon which one may spend an action. You may make a weapon attack or claws, if applicable, and a tongue lash or tail swipe without Celerity, <i>so long as you are declaring two actions to do both</i>.<br /> <br /> As far as Celerity, what I mean by &quot;they do not interact&quot; is Celerity itself supersedes the default rules on multiple attacks. It's higher on the &quot;order of operations&quot;, so to speak. With Celerity 1 or 2, you may make two attacks per round; with Celerity 3 or 4, you may make three; with Celerity 5, you may make four. The one attack you get by default, plus half your Celerity rating rounded up.<br /> <br /> When using Celerity, multiple weapons or additional natural weapons does not increase this cap. So if a character is, say, dual wielding, Celerity 1 or 2 does nothing in terms of total number of potential attacks; it isn't until Celerity 3 you can make a third attack.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we are reading the rules differently. As for declaring actions, the first time I am called to act (whcih could be in an oppenent's turn if they are attacking me with higher initiative and I have to decide whether to defend or not), I have to declare whether I am going to do only one action (so in the case of being attacked by a faster opponent, I roll my full dice pool for a dodge, block or parry without difficulty penalty) or multiple actions (in which case I roll one less dice and the difficulty is +1, and my next action in this turn will be at -2 dice and +2 diff. and so on until the difficulty of all my possible actions has reached 10, because one cannot do multiple actions at diff.10 - in theory you could roll a 10, but the rules explicitly forbid multiple actions at diff.10) or - only in the case of defense - I can declare a full defense in which case I can only take defensive actions in that turn without difficulty penalty but losing one dice per action after the first. After I have declared which of these options I take, I declare the specific action I'm going to do there and then - ex: block, dodge or parry that attack -, but I won't have to declare the other actions until it's time to take them. <br /> <br /> As for the additional attacks, they are not free actions, but they are additional ATTACK actions as exception to the default that one can only do 1 attack action per turn (and any number of other non-attack actions as long as they don't go to diff.10). They are not just &quot;options&quot; in terms of weaponry, they actually afford an extra attack, but limited to that weapon and with the normal penalties for multiple actions<br /> <br /> As for Celerity, the way my ST read it is that you don't get extra attacks with Celerity 1 or 2, you still have 1 attack, but you don't suffer the normal difficulty penalty for multiple actions for your first or second (with Celerity 2) actions, one of which can be an attack.<br /> <br /> Now let's say we are dual wielding: normally we can do 2 attacks instead of 1. This should not be changed by Celerity 1 or 2: I can still do 2 attacks, as part of multiple actions, but this time the first, thanks to Celerity, has not difficulty penalty for multiple actions. The question remains for the 2nd attack: can it benefit from Celerity too for the difficulty penalty? That is my question. At Celerity 3 I will have 3 attacks: 2 for Celerity and 1 more for dual wielding. But the same question will remain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}